    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d719">

                <pb facs="0897" n="787" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg787"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.003/583: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Nanking</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-03-31T17:00:00-05:00">March 31, 1931—5
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>18. For Minister Johnson: Your March 28, 10 a.m., and March 28, 4 p.m., from
                    Nanking.</p>
                <p>(1) <hi rendition="#italic">Transfer of jurisdiction</hi>. After the words “as
                    regards rights of appeal and in all other matters of procedure” in paragraph 2
                    (2), the Department feels it would be desirable that there be added these words,
                    “both administrative and judicial” for the purpose of having a definite
                    provision entitling American citizens to seek redress or relief from arbitrary
                    acts by administrative officials through “administrative proceedings”.</p>
                <p>In paragraphs (3) and (4) under the same heading, there are reported provisions
                    for British subjects to be subject only to modern courts of justice and police
                    courts. On account of the frequent declarations <pb facs="0898" n="788"
                        xml:id="pg788"/> of martial law in China, it is the belief of the Department
                    that it would be desirable to have in an exchange of notes the above provisions
                    made applicable at all times, even with martial law declared. It was for this
                    reason that the Department inserted the words “at all times” in its draft of
                    January 19.</p>
                <p>(2) <hi rendition="#italic">Lawyers and interpreters</hi>. Agreed minute. The
                    Department prefers having this made the subject of an exchange of notes.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
